The payload is 96 KiB of ARM Thumb BL instruction pairs, every one of which
the ARMTHUMB filter converts. It was generated by running the filter's decode
step over a buffer of identical encoded branches, so that 7-Zip's encoder turns
it back into that constant buffer and the archive stays small:

def decode(buf, ip=4):
    b = bytearray(buf)
    i = 0
    while i + 4 <= len(b):
        if (b[i+1] & 0xF8) == 0xF0 and (b[i+3] & 0xF8) == 0xF8:
            addr = ((b[i+1] & 7) << 19) | (b[i] << 11) \
                   | ((b[i+3] & 7) << 8) | b[i+2]
            addr = ((((addr << 1) - (ip + i)) & 0xFFFFFFFF) >> 1)
            b[i+1] = 0xF0 | ((addr >> 19) & 7)
            b[i]   = (addr >> 11) & 0xFF
            b[i+3] = 0xF8 | ((addr >> 8) & 7)
            b[i+2] = addr & 0xFF
            i += 2
        i += 2
    return bytes(b)

open('thumb_payload.bin', 'wb').write(decode(bytes([0, 0xF0, 0, 0xF8]) * 24576))

Being larger than the reader's 64 KiB uncompressed buffer, it also covers the
carry-over of bytes the filter cannot convert until the next chunk arrives.

The archives were then created with 7zip 26.03:

7z a -m0=ARMT -m1=Deflate -mhc=off -mtm=off test_read_format_7zip_deflate_armthumb.7z thumb_payload.bin -y
7z a -m0=ARMT -m1=LZMA -mhc=off -mtm=off test_read_format_7zip_lzma1_armthumb.7z thumb_payload.bin -y

-mtm=off keeps the result reproducible: without it the stored mtime
changes on every regeneration. The test does not check the mtime.

